        "content": "<p>o-murphy edited <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/issues/15088\">issue #15088</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Thanks for filing a bug report! Please fill out the TODOs below.</p>\n<p><strong>Note: if you want to report a security issue, please read our <a href=\"https://bytecodealliance.org/security\">security policy</a>!</strong></p>\n<h3>Test Case</h3>\n<p>No Wasm file is needed; the C API call alone reproduces it:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"C\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"cp\">#include</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"cpf\">&lt;stdio.h&gt;</span>\n<span class=\"cp\">#include</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"cpf\">&lt;wasm.h&gt;</span>\n<span class=\"cp\">#include</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"cpf\">&lt;wasmtime.h&gt;</span>\n\n<span class=\"kt\">int</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">main</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"kt\">void</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">wasm_valtype_t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">*</span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">wasmtime_wasm_valtype_v128</span><span class=\"p\">();</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">printf</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"calling wasm_valtype_kind on a v128 valtype...</span><span class=\"se\">\\n</span><span class=\"s\">\"</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">fflush</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">stdout</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">wasm_valkind_t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">wasm_valtype_kind</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"p\">);</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"cm\">/* aborts */</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">printf</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"kind = %d</span><span class=\"se\">\\n</span><span class=\"s\">\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"kt\">int</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">wasm_valtype_delete</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">return</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">;</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<h3>Steps to Reproduce</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Build the program above against the Wasmtime C API (<code>cc repro.c -lwasmtime</code>).</li>\n<li>Run it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Expected Results</h3>\n<p><code>wasm_valtype_kind()</code> does not take the host process down. <code>wasm.h</code>'s <code>wasm_valkind_t</code> has no <code>v128</code><br>\nmember, so either a documented Wasmtime-specific value is returned for <code>v128</code>, or the function's<br>\ndocumentation says it must not be called on <code>v128</code> types and points to <code>wasmtime_valtype_new()</code>,<br>\nwhich already handles <code>v128</code> (<code>wasmtime_valtype_t::V128</code>) and is what the C++ API uses.</p>\n<h3>Actual Results</h3>\n<p>The process aborts:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Rust\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">calling</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">wasm_valtype_kind</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">on</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v128</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">valtype</span><span class=\"o\">..</span><span class=\"p\">.</span>\n<span class=\"err\">`</span><span class=\"n\">support</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">for</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v128</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"err\">`</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">is</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">not</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">implemented</span>\n<span class=\"n\">Aborted</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">exit</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">code</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">134</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>The cause is the <code>// TODO</code> arm in <code>crates/c-api/src/types/val.rs</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Rust\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"k\">pub</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">extern</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s\">\"C\"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">fn</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">wasm_valtype_kind</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">vt</span><span class=\"p\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kp\">&amp;</span><span class=\"nc\">wasm_valtype_t</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">-&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nc\">wasm_valkind_t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"k\">match</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">vt</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"o\">..</span><span class=\"p\">.</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"c1\">// TODO</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"n\">ValType</span><span class=\"p\">::</span><span class=\"n\">V128</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">crate</span><span class=\"p\">::</span><span class=\"n\">abort</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"support for v128 \"</span><span class=\"p\">),</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>It is still there on <code>main</code> (81231fd, 2026-10-30).</p>\n<h3>Versions and Environment</h3>\n<p>Wasmtime version or commit: 50.0.1 (the <code>libwasmtime</code> shipped in the wasmtime-py 50.0.1 wheel); the<br>\nsame code is on <code>main</code> at 81231fd</p>\n<p>Operating system: Linux (Ubuntu 24.04)</p>\n<p>Architecture: x86_64</p>\n<h3>Extra Info</h3>\n<p>Embedders that inspect function or global types with the standard <code>wasm.h</code> API hit this as soon as a<br>\nmodule uses <code>v128</code> in a signature. wasmtime-py 50.0.1 does (its <code>ValType.__eq__</code>/<code>__str__</code> call<br>\n<code>wasm_valtype_kind()</code>), so calling an export with a <code>v128</code> parameter aborts the Python interpreter:<br>\n<a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime-py/issues/348\">bytecodealliance/wasmtime-py#348</a>. We found it while running the WebAssembly spec suite<br>\n(<code>test/core/simd</code>) through wasmtime-py as a reference implementation.</p>\n</blockquote>",
